#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Model Visualization and Issues
]

#p(3)[
  The results from the validation testing section seemed to indicate a much lower network capacity would generalize/learn better. However, the results can be misleading (as is explained in the various notes given in the repository about validation error being misleading, and other the various problems, which are given in `cyan_background.typst`, `network_fidelity_and_radiance_issues.typst` and `validation_error_explanation.typst`). However, another method to verify what was learned and how well the model learned is to attempt to visualize slices of the scene to see how well the hashgrid learnt the scene itself. This is relatively common, especially in networks which have some sort of encoding. Functionality was added to take z-slices (horizontal slices since PBRT uses Z-up) of the whole scene's bounds via `dump_slice_img`. The visualization slices of the validation configuration was tested, alongside larger configurations given in `medium.json`, `large.json` and `magenta.json` (given its name due to the radiance shown). The test error graphs are also given in `validation_error_explanation.typst`.

  The results can be seen in appendix /*!TODO: Add the images here or to the appendix*/. The images for the validation configuration look very blocky and inconsistent between slices and this is a consequence of the network being small itself. This would imply a much larger network would be better to learn from as it would have more space to be able to learn a finer representation of the scene. However, this comes with its own challenges with learning the RGB radiance instead. All of which is explained in `network_fidelity_and_radiance.typst`


]

#p(3)[
  There are a couple things to note. Firstly in `magenta.json` /*!TODO: Create magenta configuration */ the radiances look off. This is likely due to the specific hashgrid configuration itself and is explained `magenta_radiance`. Another thing is that for some, the background itself looks off, with a lot of cyan in it or just flat out blue. The reason for this is likely due to the RGB bias and the fact that it is trained off of a spectral path tracer. All of this is explained in `cyan_background.typst`.

  However, you can see that the general shape for both radiance and the throughput is similar to the actual shape of the explosion, with an expanding and contracting circular shape which would represent the fire/smoke and these slices are also consistent with each other in consecutive slices /*!TODO: Explain in which configuration those are in*/ which means that the network itself is actually learning the medium's transmittance as well as the radiance. This is the case when viewing y-slices too /*!TODO: Generate y-slices and add to report*/.

  Furthermore, you can also see that in places which did not have much training data (like empty space on the floors around the flame) that there is a lot of random noise. This makes sense for areas devoid of training data to push gradients towards learning that space. This is not inherently a bad thing as this could mean the spectral path tracer does not trace those areas and so does not need representing.
]


#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Pipeline-Model Integration and Inference
]

#p(2)[
  The integration required considering how exactly the Instant-NGP `Testbed` functionality is included, either via directly adding as a member variable or adding a smaller amount of functionality for `tiny-cuda-nn` instead. The exact details are given in `pbrt_integration.typst`, where it describes why `Testbed` was added as a member variable and other specific details on how exactly the network was integrated and how the data was prepared and unpacked.

  The basic gist is in a new `SampleMediumInteractionNGP` function which is called if a network is loaded and NGP is specified to be ran, it extracts data from `MediumInteractionWorkItem` queue and prepares them into a staging buffer to be inferred through the model. The predictions are then extracted and updates the `pixelSampleState` or passes on the calculated `beta` values by enqueue work in the proceeding kernels.

  Fortunately, other than initializing staging buffers to pass through inference input/outputs, `r_l` and `r_u` and beta before/after and lambda before/after to store results from the network, all the changes required are just local to `SampleMediumInteractionNGP` and don't require change in other areas. This due to the wavefront architecture design leading to each kernel function being independent and isolated from the others, leading to changes to this/*!TODO improve this one area*/

  However, due to the way the network is slotted into the wavefront architecture, it assumes the ray passes through the entire `tMax` distance into the medium and either leaves or hits another medium. Thus, no medium scattering occurs within the current medium so no `MediumScatteringWorkItem` is enqueued from the new `SampleMediumInteractionNGP` unlike the native way. There are also a couple side effects of this. Firstly, if the network detects emission at in front of the smoke, then it will cause it to skip the smoke behind it and not have dark non-transmittive cloud behind it and the opposite is true, if the network detects non-emittin low transmittance smoke in front of the emitting fire, it will not output any emission at all as it skips straight through. A more in depth exploration of this is given in `radiance_transmission_slice_mismatch.typst`. Another consequence of this volume skip through is that any shadow rays that would have been enqueued during `SampleMediumScattering` does not occur so indirect light or self-shadowing of the volume cannot occur. Overall, there are quite a few limitations that need to be considered during the evaluation of the model and the inference results.
]




#heading(depth: 1)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Results and Evaluation
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Results
]

#p(2)[
  Below are the results comparing the 3 metrics that were proposed. Note, this includes spectral network results that are explained below in @Ablation_Study
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Visual Quality
]

#p(3)[
  In terms of visual quality there has been partial success. Firstly, radiance inference for the RGB networks was poor. It failed to show any meaningful yellow/orange flame and this issue was caused by a number of factors from RGB bias from spectral conversion as well as limitations in integration and the impact of the volume scattering skip. When looking at the slice visualizations however, there is evidence to suggest that it did learn the shape of the fire and emissive sections of the scene but this could not showcased sufficiently in the inference. When considering throughput, the larger networks, though not perfect, did a better at learning the general shape of the smoke and the finer details, whereas smaller networks learned which areas generally had lower throughput but failed to learn the higher frequency areas like the smaller offshoots and wisps of smoke near the edges. If done again, there needs to be a bigger consideration of exactly how to deal with volume scattering being skipped as it is clear the networks have learnt a scene representation of radiance and throughput but was failed to be actualized during path tracing. In terms of learning novel views, it did similarly poorly. This was likely due to the training set itself not being varied enough or having too few orientations around the scene. Part of it was also explained in `validation_error_explanation.typst` where a lot of rays just hit the outer sphere instead of the smoke. Similarly to how Monte Carlo estimation tries to match samples with areas of higher importance, the training set would require samples of areas of higher importance.

  For spectral networks, it was more successful. It was able to get a high quality reconstruction of the original view and in some ways performed better with less grainy and better blended wisps on the edges. On novel views there were some which were poor but others which it did well in reconstructing likely only let down by the training data. Therefore, overall this ablation study was successful in showcasing the performance of spectral networks.
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Computation Time
]

#p(3)[
  Native path is:  for 3 iterations
  good_12_19_1-38_tAfter_linear is:  for 3 iterations
  The computation time is calculated on Max inference batch size is: 780000 with padded max batch: 780032

  The computation time is on average $3times$ faster for the NGP route as opposed to the native rendering approach. The below table/in the appendix showcases this. For the native PBRT path, it took approximately 3 seconds over 3 different runs as opposed to the NGP route which took only about 1 second over 3 different renders. This is staggeringly faster and showcases even via a relatively simple and naive integration, it has massive time saves.

  #timings-graph
]

#heading(depth: 3)[
  #set text(size: 10pt)
  Memory Usage
]

#p(3)[
  In terms of memory, the network itself does not require that much memory, only on the order of magnitude of $approx 10 "MB"$. However, what takes up more space is the naive implementation of the integration via the multiple staging buffers which are max queue size. Below/in appendix showcases the calculated amount of extra memory required for different configurations


  You can the factors that have the most impact on memory usage are slot map/inference staging buffers, particularly for larger and larger `maxInferenceBatchSize`.

  #memory-table
]


#p(2)[
  /*!TODO: Get results on the time it takes to complete as well as the amount of memory it takes*/
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Evaluating RGB-based Networks
]


#p(2)[
  The resulting images, as seen in the appendix, paint a clear picture. There are clear issues with the fidelity and quality of emission and throughput. This is partially due to the restrictions given above of emission being behind throughput and volume emission having a corresponding throughput close to zero making it more difficult to render emissive areas. /*!TODO: give a better explanation*/ There may be some way to ensure the radiance is rendered regardless of whether the network thinks its in front or behind the network however this project will only consider a relatively simple integration of the model into PBRT as this would require significant divergence from the original algorithm. The throughput and radiance mismatch may also be alleviated with a higher fidelity hashgrid, but this comes with its own problems of not learning radiance as shown in `missing_radiance_in_larger_networks.typst`.

  Thus, from hereon, we will consider using a different approach. We will consider evaluation of throughput only as opposed to also considering radiance as this gets a better idea of how well the network learnt the shape of the explosion/smoke and can be more easily visualized as opposed to radiance which would have a throughput of close to 0. Through this, we can see how the throughput will vary across the medium from thinner to more denser areas.

  The results are given in appendix /*!TODO: Give the figures in the appendix*/
]

#p(2)[
  The results for throughput only paint a different picture. For larger hashgrids, though not perfect, the overall shape and some of the more finer details of the smoke can be seen. For the smaller `n_levels` network, some of the areas that had much lower throughput, particularly above the area of the fire itself, had a similar shape with low throughput. In the larger networks, there is an explicit outline of the smoke that matches the original at least for the original/principle view and though there is a lot of noise elsewhere, this can be explained via the RGB bias given in `RGB_bias.typst` leading to empty space being learnt to have low throughput for red areas as well. Despite this noise, there is still clear areas that match the shape.

  However, when considering novel views, the network does poorly. This may likely be due to the fact that the training dataset may have had more bias towards directions in the original directions or rays hitting empty space or the bounding volume sphere and thus failed to learn other perspectives. This offers discussion for improvement on the training method employed in this project to be more uniform and have more orientations to train. /*!TODO: expand more on this area*/
]



#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Ablation study: Spectral-based Networks
]<Ablation_Study>

#p(3)[
  This begs the question, is there a fundamental issue with learning just RGB radiance values? An argument can be made that there is an issue as converting from the higher dimensionality of 4 spectrum values that PBRT samples to just 3 RGB values inherently loses some information. This is due to the conversion both accounting for sensor and RGB colorspace conversion, inherently making the network tied to whichever colorspace it was trained on, but also due to the fact that many wavelengths for green overlap with red and blue as it attempts to simulate how the cones in human eyes work, potentially giving a heavier bias towards green without taking into account particular sensor sensitivity.

  Thus, a small experiment can be conducted to see the results of training only throughput using the 4 spectral throughput values instead of 3 RGB values. However, due to the nature of the network not guaranteeing the same 4 wavelengths sampled during volume scattering/medium interactions (explained earlier about why the project used RGB) this requires a modification. To conduct this ablation study, PBRT was changed to predictly sample the same 4 wavelengths of /*!TODO: Give wavelengths sampled here*/ in order to be deterministic about the wavelengths. Future work could be done to have the specific wavelengths logged and used as input to the network as well, but for now we will consider only the given 4 wavelengths. Unfortunately, without the foresight to also record the position of the camera in the random orientations for the original training dataset, this network was trained on the original position and 4 other random orientations different from the training set. So potentially not a 1-1 comparison but still gives a good way to evalute spectral network performance.

  The configuration used is given in `chosen_config_spectral.json` which is similar to the larger configurations used earlier as those were the ones that gave the best reconstruction but also because this spectral network is not trained on radiance which is what larger models struggled with. The results are given below/in the appendix
]

#p(3)[
  As you can see, for the original view it had an almost perfect reconstruction, matching even the smaller and finer wisps of smoke that the original had. Compared to the original, the more wispier parts also appear less grainy/noisy and does a better job at smoothly interpolating from higher to lower throughput compared to the native approach which is expected from a neural network as it tends to prefer a smoother interpolation/function to learn. Overall, for the original/principal view, it does a very good job at reconstruction.

  However, looking at the novel orientations, it appears there are issues but not as bad as the RGB network version. The first novel view is pretty much a mess with only a very small number of pixels having the correct throughput and mostly looks like noise, so for this view it didn't quite learn enough about the volume. However the second novel view did a lot better. Though there were quite a few areas which had a throughput of 1, not matching the original, the overall shape matched and had the same distinct outline of the smoke. This could have been a limitation of the size or variety of the training set but this second view shows potential.
]

#heading(depth: 1)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Reflection and Discussion of Future Work
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Critical Reflection
]


#p(2)[
  Looking back at the results we can break down how successful the this project has been across the 3 /*!TODO: Get a better name for it*/ metrics proposed in Section 1.3
]


#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Future Work
]

#p(2)[
  Due to the deficiencies that resulted from performing the RGB conversion from the spectral path tracer, future work should focus on training on spectral data and particuarly looking at utilizing spectral networks for the radiance as it looks like it may be able to perform well to predict radiance as well as the weighted throughput ablation study seems to do well. Furthermore, more work should be done to look at a better strategy for integration, specifically about how to consider compensating for the loss of the medium scattering kernel/call or even about how to potentially incorporate that into the network as well which may require a different approach to take into account the non-deterministic nature of Monte Carlo estimation. This may be in the form of another Fully Fused MLP that runs along side or after the MLP that operates on calculating medium interaction. The medium interaction network can then be modified to output positions and event scattering data that is fed into the scattering network to predict the radiance/throughput for those scattered positions/events. In this way, the medium interaction network is more closely aligned with what the original kernel does which is predict scattering events whereas the scattering network actually calculates the effects of those scattering events. This allows for the learned radiance to accurately and correctly be applied in the tracing that could not have occurred with the proposed network in this project. However this would require signficant modification to Instant-NGP in order to train an architecture like this. Another thing that may be useful to consider is adding additional inputs for the specific wavelengths sampled, to account for the fact that native spectral path tracing operates on sampled wavelengths that may not be consistent between pixel samples.
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Conclusion
]

#p(2)[
  Overall, the project has had its up and downs but was partially successful in achieving the project objectives. The project was able to successfully build a hybrid pipeline, train a neural network, complete a neural network integration, though rudimentary and with many areas to improve upon, and able to evaluate the performance across 3 metrics. The viability of neural networks in spectral path tracing has also been shown to have some promise. There have points raised which would improve this work, particularly on the integration, and there is promise that pursuing spectral networks, increasing their complexity or becoming more specialized may result in better renderings and should be investigated in future work.
]



